  "body": "Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object fails to distinguish two replies that its documented rule requires it to tell apart. The rule I am reading is: “The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.” The first reply text is exactly the following four lines: a line with three backticks followed by the word python, a line with {\"a\":1}, a line with three backticks, and a line with {\"b\":2}. The second reply text is exactly the one line {\"b\":2}. For the first reply, the rule requires the answer {\"a\":1}, because the reply contains a code fence (the python fence) holding that object, and that is the last fenced object. For the second reply, the rule requires the answer {\"b\":2}, because there is no code fence, so the last top-level object is {\"b\":2}. The function, however, gives the same answer for both: it returns {\"b\":2}, because its fence regex only recognizes fences with no language or with json/JSON, so the python fence is not collected, and the top-level scan then picks the last top-level object, which is {\"b\":2} in both replies. I would be wrong if recover_json_object on the first reply returns {\"a\":1} rather than {\"b\":2}, or if the documented rule is read to exclude non-JSON language fences from the phrase “code fence.”"
